## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single provider that feeds ALL content to the React tree (236+ consumers via useContent → useContentstackContext). Currently it only accepts static defaultContent via props and freezes it with useMemo — there is no mechanism to receive or merge live/draft content updates from Contentstack's preview API. Live preview requires this provider to support dynamic content updates that flow to every consumer automatically.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the useMemo-only approach with useState initialized from defaultContent, and add a Contentstack live-preview listener (onEntryChange from the Contentstack SDK) that merges incoming preview data into state. The existing ContentstackFactory in src/services/contentstack.ts already wraps the Contentstack SDK client — add the live preview initialization there (via client.livePreview or Stack.livePreview) and expose an onEntryChange callback. In ContentstackProvider, subscribe to that callback and call setContent to merge the updated entry into the existing content object. This way all 236+ useContent consumers automatically receive live preview updates without any component-level changes.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- A tester edits a draft content entry in the Contentstack CMS and, without reloading or restarting the running application, sees the updated text rendered on the application page where that content is displayed.
- After a draft change is saved in the CMS, the updated content becomes visible in the application without requiring a new build, redeploy, or manual refresh by the user.
- Every location in the running application that displays the edited piece of content shows the updated draft version, not just a single page or component.
- Content entries that have not been edited in the CMS continue to display their previously published text unchanged in the running application.
- A tester navigating between pages of the running application continues to see the latest draft content displayed consistently across those pages.
- While live preview is active, the application continues to render all CMS-provided content without blank sections, errors, or missing text on the displayed screens.

